<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</PackageId>
    <Authors>PRT</Authors>
    <Description>Nexus V2 - Layer 06 Product Core - Nexus.ProductCore.Core</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    W8B / F-23 — CROSS-CONSUMER BINARY REPRODUCIBILITY.

    THE DEFECT. Forge and Nexus.Developer both compile this project from source, through their
    own build graphs (DevBridge/src/SharedDevelopmentControl.props and src/SharedDevelopmentControl.props
    respectively). The Developer cross-host proofs compare the two consumers' copies of this
    assembly BYTE FOR BYTE, and on 2026-09-17 a mismatch was reported as "STALE FORGE HARNESS, not a
    host divergence" — the guard working, but the cause unlocated. W8D carried it as F-23.

    THE CAUSE, measured rather than inferred. The compiler inputs are identical for an identical
    property set: dumping csc's own command line via -p:ProvideCommandLineArgs=true and diffing the
    two consumer builds yields ZERO differences across 234 arguments. What differs is the PROPERTY
    SET — and the one that bites is $(NexusPlatformRoot), whose spelling each consumer supplies. A
    build with D:\NEXUS\... and a build with D:\Nexus\... are the same tree to the file system and
    two different strings to the compiler, and the path is embedded (the assembly's source
    documents and its debug-directory linkage follow it). That is why the two consumers diverged
    while each was internally reproducible: the same property set always produced the same bytes.

    THE FIX IS HERE, NOT IN THE CONSUMERS. Owner direction: "Do not solve it by making each
    consumer independently compensate." A PathMap set in a CONSUMER's props would not work in any
    case — a <PropertyGroup> there sets the property on the importing project and does not flow into
    this referenced one; only command-line globals do. Setting it in this project maps the paths
    where the compilation actually happens, so the remedy cannot be applied to one consumer and not
    the other, and cannot drift.

    WHAT THIS DOES. /pathmap rewrites the absolute prefix of every source document the compiler
    records to /_/nexus/, so the output no longer depends on where the tree is checked out or how
    the path is spelled. Deterministic is already on by default in the SDK and is set explicitly
    here so the property's absence can never be mistaken for its being satisfied.

    Conditioned on PathMap being empty so an explicit -p:PathMap=... from a command line still wins;
    a hard assignment would silently override a caller who knows better.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>

    <!--
      DeterministicSourcePaths is the half that PathMap does NOT cover, and finding that out was
      the point of this task. PathMap rewrites the compiler's source DOCUMENTS; it does not touch
      the SourceLink mapping, which the SDK derives separately from the repository root and embeds
      in the PDB as {"documents":{"<absolute repo path>/*":"<url>/*"}}. Measured: with PathMap alone
      but DeterministicSourcePaths off, the two consumers still produced different PDBs — and
      therefore different DLLs, because the debug directory carries the PDB identity — because one
      spelled the root D:\NEXUS\... and the other D:\Nexus\.... Roslyn's deterministic timestamp is
      a hash of the whole compilation, options included, so a one-character difference anywhere in
      those inputs changes the output.

      With DeterministicSourcePaths on, the SDK maps repository paths to the fixed /_/ root instead
      of the machine's absolute path, so the SourceLink mapping stops depending on where the tree
      is or how its path is spelled. This is set HERE, in the shared project, and not in either
      consumer's props: a <PropertyGroup> in a consumer cannot reach this project's compilation in
      any case (only command-line globals flow down a ProjectReference), and even if it could, the
      Owner's direction is explicit — "Do not solve it by making each consumer independently
      compensate."
    -->
    <DeterministicSourcePaths>true</DeterministicSourcePaths>

    <!--
      W10.0A CANONICAL INTEGRATION — THE DEPTH HERE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT COSMETIC.

      _NexusSharedRoot is the absolute prefix that PathMap rewrites to /_/nexus/, so it MUST be
      the directory whose spelling varies between consumers. That directory is the repository
      root, and this project sat at src/Nexus.ProductCore.Core/ (two levels) on the historical
      flat line — hence "..\..\". The canonical layout moves it to
      src/06-SharedPlatform/Nexus.ProductCore.Core/ (three levels), so the same repository root
      is now "..\..\..\".

      Leaving the old depth would have been a SILENT regression of F-23 rather than a build
      error: PathMap would have mapped D:\Nexus\Platform\src\ to /_/nexus/, leaving the actual
      repository root — the string the consumers disagree about — in every source document and
      in the PDB's SourceLink mapping. The two consumers would have diverged again, byte for
      byte, exactly as before, and this time the remedy would have been sitting right here
      looking correct. Both shared projects are at the same depth for the same reason.

      THE SEPARATORS BELOW ARE "/" ON PURPOSE. System.IO.Path.GetFullPath treats a backslash as a
      separator on Windows and as an ordinary filename character on Unix, so the same "..\..\..\"
      that normalises correctly on a developer's machine resolves to a LITERAL directory called
      "..\..\..\" on the Linux CI runner — no error, just a PathMap key that never matches and a
      SourceRoot that does not exist. F-23 would then be silently un-remedied in the very build the
      estate treats as the gate. "/" is accepted by GetFullPath on both platforms and normalises to
      the same absolute path, so the value — and therefore the compiled output — is unchanged on
      Windows. Measured: the assembly hash is identical either way.
    -->
    <_NexusSharedRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../'))</_NexusSharedRoot>
    <PathMap Condition="'$(PathMap)' == ''">$(_NexusSharedRoot)=/_/nexus/</PathMap>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- SourceRoot is what DeterministicSourcePaths maps FROM; without it the SDK cannot produce
         the deterministic mapping and leaves the absolute path in place. -->
    <SourceRoot Include="$(_NexusSharedRoot)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    W8D-R4 TASK 2 + TASK 3. Layer 06 SHARED PLATFORM - the canonical DevelopmentControl
    reader/writer/locking implementation, moved here from Forge per Owner decision R4-01,
    and exposed through the shared contract surface in Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts.

    DEPENDENCY POSTURE, stated exactly rather than aspirationally.

    TASK 2 committed this project with zero references of any kind and it built clean; that
    state is recorded in git. TASK 3 added the single ProjectReference below, because the
    contract interfaces a caller consumes live in the contracts assembly and this assembly
    implements them. The dependency is one-way and points at the contract.

    What that costs: Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts carries a PackageReference on
    Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions, so this project now inherits it
    transitively. Measured, that package IS present in the local NuGet cache (10.0.11), so
    the restore resolves offline.

    What that does NOT cost: the BOOTSTRAP_SAFE property LAYER_MODEL.md section 06 requires -
    "zero-IO, no dependency on this layer's own product-specific types - Forge may consume it
    even when Nexus's runtime/Shared Platform is down". DependencyInjection.Abstractions is a
    compile-time abstraction with no runtime service behind it; nothing here contacts a host,
    a database or a feed at run time. There is still NO ClosedXML reference and no OOXML SDK
    reference: the workbook reading is ZipArchive + XDocument over the BCL. The moved sources
    use only BCL namespaces.

    Adding any FURTHER reference to this file is an architectural change, not a build tweak:
    it is the difference between an assembly Forge can always consume and one it cannot. See
    W8DR4_COMPONENT_OWNERSHIP.md and W8DR4_SHARED_CONTRACT.md.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts\Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    W10.0A CANONICAL INTEGRATION — FORWARD OBLIGATION, recorded here because this is the file a
    consumer reads.

    Forge's and Nexus.Developer's shared-control import builds its ProjectReferences from
    $(NexusPlatformRoot)\src\<Project>\..., which is the FLAT layout. On this canonical line the
    projects are one level deeper, so when those consumer adaptations are merged their paths must
    be re-pointed:
        $(NexusPlatformRoot)\src\Nexus.ProductCore.Core\      -> ...\src\06-SharedPlatform\Nexus.ProductCore.Core\
        $(NexusPlatformRoot)\src\Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts\ -> ...\src\06-SharedPlatform\Nexus.ProductCore.Contracts\
        $(NexusPlatformRoot)\src\Nexus.Governance.Core\       -> ...\src\03-Governance\Nexus.Governance.Core\
        $(NexusPlatformRoot)\src\Nexus.Governance.Contracts\  -> ...\src\03-Governance\Nexus.Governance.Contracts\

    MEASURED, not assumed: as of this integration the live Forge and Developer working trees contain
    no SharedDevelopmentControl.props and no occurrence of NexusPlatformRoot — the W8B/W8C/W8D
    adaptation exists only on unmerged lane branches. So this is a forward obligation for whoever
    merges those lanes, not a live breakage. It is recorded rather than fixed because changing
    another repository is outside this milestone's scope.
  -->

</Project>
